💡 Result:

If you open GORM with the sqlite in-memory DSN ":memory:", each separate database connection gets its own independent in-memory database, so data won’t be visible to other connections (and therefore a second gorm.Open won’t see tables/data created by the first) [1]. To share one in-memory database across multiple connections, you must use a SQLite URI filename with a named in-memory database and shared cache, e.g. file:memdb1?mode=memory&cache=shared (or file::memory:?cache=shared). SQLite’s own docs describe that ":memory:" is always private to the connection that opened it, but a URI filename can create a shareable/named in-memory database across connections [1]. Practical guidance for GORM tests/usage: 1) Prefer a shared in-memory URI name instead of ":memory:": - example: "file:memdb1?mode=memory&cache=shared" [1] 2) If you still observe “empty”/missing data or “table not found” with shared in-memory, ensure you are not using more than one physical connection. - GORM uses database/sql underneath and maintains a connection pool; you can tune it via sqlDB.SetMaxOpenConns(...) after db.DB [2]. - For SQLite, limiting to a single open connection is a common mitigation because multiple connections can cause issues with in-memory sharing and locking/concurrency [3]. Connection pool setting (GORM): - After gorm.Open(...), do: sqlDB, err:= gormDB.DB // SetMaxOpenConns sets the maximum number of open connections sqlDB.SetMaxOpenConns(1) This uses the standard database/sql pooling hook that GORM documents for connection pooling [2]. In short: - ":memory:" will not share state between multiple connections [1]. - Use "file:<name>?mode=memory&cache=shared" to share state across connections [1]. - If you need deterministic single-connection behavior, call sqlDB.SetMaxOpenConns(1) [2], especially in test scenarios that create multiple gorm.Open/db instances [3].

Citations:

- 1: https://sqlite.org/inmemorydb.html
- 2: https://gorm.io/docs/connecting_to_the_database.html
- 3: https://github.com/mattn/go-sqlite3/issues/204

---

